# {{.Name}}

Generated with GoAnvilInstaller. Go 1.24 or newer is required.

## Run

Run commands from this project root. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set your
{{.Database}} connection settings (passwords belong in `.env`, not source control).
For server databases, create the database before running commands.

```sh
go mod tidy
go run . migrate up
go run . serve
```

GET `/health` returns `{"status":"ok"}`. Startup connects to the configured database;
the health endpoint itself reports process health, not database readiness.
Stop with Ctrl+C. Configuration loads once: defaults < env file < process env.
`APP_ENV_FILE` selects a required file; an empty value disables file loading.

{{if .FrameworkPath}}This checkout uses a local framework replace directive. Remove it with
`go mod edit -dropreplace={{.FrameworkModule}}` after publishing the framework and
selecting a real version with `go get {{.FrameworkModule}}@VERSION`.
{{else}}The framework dependency must be published at {{.FrameworkModule}} {{.FrameworkVersion}}.
For unpublished development, initialize with `--framework-path=/path/to/GoAnvil`.
{{end}}
## Generate

```sh
anvil make:model Todo --fields=Content:string,Done:bool
anvil make:migration --up --down --model=Todo
go run . migrate up
```

Models go into `models/`; application repositories and services go under `internal/`.
Generators find `.anvil.json` from nested directories. They refuse overwrites.
The migration generator reads the Go struct and targets the database selected at
initialization, recorded in `.anvil.json`. Changing DB_DRIVER does not translate
existing SQL: review and replace migrations when changing database dialects.
Table names default to the snake_case model name plus `s`; use `--table` for
irregular plurals. Supported fields: string, bool, int, int64, uint64 (except SQLite),
float64, time.Time, []byte, and nullable pointers. ID must be int or int64.
Tags do not customize column names. Edit generated SQL for indexes, constraints,
defaults, relationships, or schema changes; this generates initial CREATE/DROP only.
It does not generate CRUD routes, repositories, or ORM adapters.

The runner records applied filenames in `goanvil_migrations`. `migrate up` applies
pending files; `migrate down` reverses **all** recorded files, dropping their tables
and data. Down filenames must match up filenames. Review SQL before running it.
Run one migrator at a time. DDL rollback depends on the engine; MySQL can commit
DDL before a migration fails. There are no checksums or automatic schema diffs.

Use public interfaces and `anvil.Bind[T]` / `anvil.Resolve[T]` for dependencies.
Repositories take context first and call `database.FromContext` per operation.
Services choose transactions; HTTP middleware commits buffered successes and
rolls back errors, error statuses, panics, and canceled open transactions.
Streaming responses require explicit finalization before sending data.

